# SELinux policy for pam_ssoossh.
#
# The module opens an outbound HTTPS connection to ssoosshd from inside
# whatever process is running the PAM stack. On a distribution with the
# targeted policy -- Red Hat Enterprise Linux and its rebuilds, Fedora --
# some of those processes are confined domains that the shipped policy
# does not permit to talk to the network at all, because nothing in the
# base system ever asked them to.
#
# The case every EL site hits is console login. /bin/login runs as
# local_login_t, which has no outbound TCP, so pam_ssoossh fails there
# while working perfectly from sudo:
#
#     avc: denied { name_connect } for pid=1667 comm="login" dest=443
#       scontext=system_u:system_r:local_login_t:s0-s0:c0.c1023
#       tcontext=system_u:object_r:http_port_t:s0 tclass=tcp_socket
#
# sudo is unaffected because it runs unconfined. sshd certificate
# authentication is unaffected because that path is TrustedUserCAKeys plus
# AuthorizedPrincipalsCommand and never enters PAM at all.
#
# Built with checkpolicy and policycoreutils, deliberately not with
# selinux-policy-devel's refpolicy Makefile: this needs no interfaces, and
# the plain module form builds from a much smaller set of packages.
#
#     make selinux              # or, by hand:
#     checkmodule -M -m -o pam_ssoossh.mod pam_ssoossh.te
#     semodule_package -o pam_ssoossh.pp -m pam_ssoossh.mod
#     semodule -i pam_ssoossh.pp
#
# The output basename has to match the module name declared below;
# checkmodule refuses otherwise ("Module name pam_ssoossh is different
# than the output base filename ..."). So this file, the `module` line and
# every artifact built from them share one name, and the Makefile derives
# the output names rather than repeating them.
#
# SCOPE. Only domains with a confirmed AVC are listed. Every entry below
# was reproduced on a real host and re-run in permissive mode to confirm
# nothing denies *after* the name_connect -- that second run is what says
# one rule is the whole answer for a domain rather than only its first
# wall. Adding a domain on reasoning alone would grant it network access
# it may not need, so domains we merely suspect are named in
# pam_ssoossh(8) as untested rather than allowed here.
#
# sshd_t is confirmed but deliberately absent. The denial is real --
# Oracle Linux 9.8, targeted policy, enforcing, comm="sshd-session"
# (OpenSSH 9.8 split the per-connection worker out, so an audit search for
# comm="sshd" finds nothing), with a permissive re-run showing a single
# AVC and nothing denying after the name_connect. It is not allowed here
# because the sshd service does not work for an unrelated reason: the
# approval URL never reaches the client while the module waits, since
# OpenSSH accumulates PAM_TEXT_INFO until a prompt or the end of the
# conversation, and this module only ever displays. See pam_ssoossh(8),
# SERVICES. Shipping the rule now would grant a domain network access for
# a path nobody can use, and the confirmation reaches only the connect and
# the polling after it -- the post-approval code could not be exercised,
# because the approval cannot be completed over ssh. When the service
# works, the whole path gets one pass and the rule goes in on that.
#
# crond_t is not one of those: it is not applicable. cronie calls
# pam_acct_mgmt, pam_open_session and pam_setcred but never
# pam_authenticate, so an auth-type module in /etc/pam.d/crond is
# unreachable code -- confirmed by running a job with the module in that
# stack, enforcing and then permissive, with no denial in either window.
# That stops being true the moment pam_ssoossh grows an account or
# session entry point, so anyone adding one should retest crond rather
# than inherit this note.
#
# PORTS. http_port_t is the label on 80, 443, 8080 and the other ports the
# base policy already calls HTTP. An ssoosshd on a port outside that set
# is denied identically, and the fix is to label the port rather than to
# widen this module:
#
#     semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 9443
#
# This grants outbound HTTP connect and nothing else -- no file access, no
# capabilities, no ability to listen. The domains it names can already
# resolve names through nsswitch.

module pam_ssoossh 1.0.0;

require {
    type local_login_t;
    type http_port_t;
    class tcp_socket name_connect;
}

# Console login: /bin/login. Confirmed on Oracle Linux 9.8, targeted
# policy, SELinux enforcing.
#
# That confirmation loaded the module by hand, with `semodule -i`, which
# installs at the default priority 400. The rpm's postinstall uses
# `semodule -X 200 -i` instead -- the vendor slot, so that a site's own
# module of the same name at 400 still wins, which is not a decision a
# package should take from an operator. Nothing has exercised the priority
# 200 path on an enforcing host. The rule is confirmed; the packaged
# delivery of it is not, and those are different claims.
allow local_login_t http_port_t:tcp_socket name_connect;
